   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] pairs error",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Sorry to just now be getting to this. The problem is that theta_4 was commented out of the model and whatever version of rstan you used had a bug in pairs about catching non-existent parameters that somehow got fixed along the way. In any event,<br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">pairs</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">fit</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> pars </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;theta_1&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;theta_2&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;theta_3&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><br>should work but for diagnostic purposes, you usually want to add &quot;lp__&quot; to pars and condition = &quot;something&quot; to the arguments, where &quot;something&quot; is (a unique abbreviation of) the name of one of the extra parameters like accept_stat__ .<br><br>Ben</div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
